 ## Overview
 
-Voxel Mesh Generation with Face Culling is a CPU-based surface extraction method that converts a 3D voxel grid into a static triangle mesh by iterating through voxels and emitting geometry only for faces adjacent to empty space (air) or transparent blocks [S2, S3, S4, unofficial]. It solves the problem of excessive vertex and index counts in naive voxel rendering by hiding internal faces shared between two solid blocks [S4, unofficial]. Unlike [Naive Voxel Meshing](2376-naive-voxel-meshing.md), which renders every voxel as a full cube, this method reduces draw calls and vertex count by culling occluded geometry [S4, unofficial]. It differs from [Visible Block Faces](2268-visible-block-faces.md) by typically operating on fixed-size chunks with explicit adjacency bitmasks rather than purely visibility-driven culling per frame, and from [GPU Marching Cubes (Face-based)](3674-gpu-marching-cubes-face-based.md) by being a deterministic, static mesh generation approach suitable for CPU-side updates [S1, S3, S4, unofficial].
+Voxel Mesh Generation with Face Culling is a CPU-based surface extraction method that converts a 3D voxel grid into a static triangle mesh by iterating through voxels and emitting geometry only for faces adjacent to empty space (air) or transparent blocks [S2, S3, S4, unofficial]. It solves the problem of excessive vertex and index counts in naive voxel rendering by hiding internal faces shared between two solid blocks [S4, unofficial]. Unlike [Naive Voxel Meshing](2376-naive-voxel-meshing.md), which renders every voxel as a full cube, this method reduces draw calls and vertex count by culling occluded geometry [S4, unofficial]. It differs from [Visible Block Faces](2268-visible-block-faces.md) by typically operating on fixed-size chunks with explicit adjacency checks rather than purely visibility-driven culling per frame, and from [GPU Marching Cubes (Face-based)](3674-gpu-marching-cubes-face-based.md) by being a deterministic, static mesh generation approach suitable for CPU-side updates [S1, S3, S4, unofficial].
 
